WEBVTT

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:15.250
<v Ira Glass>The question isn't, can you go home again? The question is, do you want to? From the day he left the small town he grew up in, David thought he did. He left Allendale, South Carolina for Princeton, then law school.
<v Ira Glass>问题不在于你是否能再回到家乡。问题在于，你还想回去吗？大卫离开他从小长大的小镇那天起，他就以为自己想。他离开南卡罗来纳州的艾伦代尔，去了普林斯顿，然后又去读了法学院。

00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:24.450
<v David Sneed>I really see myself as being a black Matlock or something like that. I mean, I'm into that idea. I'm into the idea of being the lawyer in town.
<v David Sneed>我真的觉得自己像是个黑人版的马特洛克之类的人物。我是说，我很认同那个想法。我很喜欢成为镇上律师的那个主意。

00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:41.210
<v Ira Glass>Just recently, he was offered a job with a country lawyer back home. And so he and his wife have to decide, what are they going to do? She grew up on a farm outside Allendale. They were childhood sweethearts. And deciding where to go, it's the biggest question in their marriage.
<v Ira Glass>就在最近，家乡的一位乡村律师给他提供了一份工作。于是他和妻子不得不决定，他们该怎么办？她在艾伦代尔外面的一个农场长大。他们是青梅竹马。决定去哪里，这是他们婚姻中最大的问题。

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:59.980
<v Letta Sneed>I try to conceptualize myself. Could I really live in a community that small? Could I live in a town with two traffic lights? And I could not see it. I could not visualize it. And I think that only a couple of weeks ago, I told David that I could definitely never live in a town like Allendale.
<v Letta Sneed>我试着给自己做个构想。我真的能住在那么小的社区里吗？我能住在只有两个红绿灯的镇上吗？我无法想象。我无法在脑海中浮现那个画面。我想大概就在几周前，我告诉大卫，我绝对永远无法住在像艾伦代尔那样的镇上。

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:07.550
<v David Sneed>That's pretty strong language, Lee.
<v David Sneed>这话说的够重的，李。

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:20.130
<v Ira Glass>In a sense, their dispute comes down to a difference over how they view a central fact of small town life, that feeling that comes when you know everybody knows you and knows your business. He likes that feeling.
<v Ira Glass>某种程度上，他们的分歧归结于对小镇生活一个核心事实的不同看法，那就是当你觉得每个人都认识你、都知道你的私事时所产生的那种感觉。他喜欢那种感觉。

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:54.080
<v David Sneed>People knowing your business or wanting to know your business, I mean, it sounds like a horrible thing. But that, whatever, nosiness, in some respects, keeps you in line. People knowing that my car was potentially parked at Letta's house at a time that it shouldn't have been keeps me in line. I mean, so you don't do that. You don't do the things that you know the neighbor next door can report back to your mom. You just have to be a good boy. And that's the way that I see myself in Allendale. I'm Allendale's little, good boy.
<v David Sneed>人们了解你的私事或者想要打听你的私事，听起来像是件可怕的事。但那种，不管怎么说，窥探欲，在某些方面，会让你循规蹈矩。人们知道我的车在不该出现的时候停在莱塔家门口，这会让我循规蹈矩。我是说，你不会那么做。你不会做那些你知道邻居会向你妈告状的事。你只需要做个乖孩子。这就是我在艾伦代尔看待自己的方式。我是艾伦代尔的小乖孩。

00:01:54.080 --> 00:02:23.390
<v Letta Sneed>And I think that I always bucked against being a good girl. I mean, don't get me wrong, I was not a mischievous child. I didn't have any discipline problems. But I think that, at a very early age, I was fiercely an individualist. And I think that, when you want to take stake-out, and behave in your own way, and have a personality that may not be assimilated to everyone else's, it's just painful in a small town, because it sticks out.
<v Letta Sneed>我觉得我总是反抗去做个乖女孩。别误会，我不是个淘气的孩子。我没有任何纪律问题。但我认为，很小的时候，我就有着强烈的个人主义。我认为，当你想要特立独行，以自己的方式行事，并且拥有一个可能无法同化于他人的个性时，在小镇里是很痛苦的，因为那太显眼了。

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:54.930
<v Ira Glass>Her views on abortion and race relations didn't square with the prevalent opinions in Allendale. The speech she gave as high school valedictorian, a speech critical of the school system, was a minor town scandal. People apparently talked about it for months. And so, for now, they're stuck living in Manhattan, trying to decide if they see small town life as cozy or as claustrophobic.
<v Ira Glass>她对堕胎和种族关系的看法与艾伦代尔普遍的观点不符。她作为高中毕业代表致辞时，批评了学校制度，这在镇上成了一桩小丑闻。人们显然谈论了几个月。所以，目前他们还困在曼哈顿生活，试图决定他们是将小镇生活视为舒适还是压抑。

00:02:54.930 --> 00:03:19.930
<v Ira Glass>Well, from WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life . I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose a theme, bring you a variety of stories on that theme. Today's program, Small Towns, the claustrophobia and freedom and yearning for small towns. Act One, Country Mouse, City Mouse, a family moves from a tiny town to the big city, or what seems to them, anyway, like the big city.
<v Ira Glass>这里是芝加哥WBEZ电台，《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周我们的节目都会选择一个主题，为您带来围绕该主题的各种故事。今天的节目是“小镇”，关于小镇的压抑、自由和向往。第一幕，“乡村老鼠，城市老鼠”，一个家庭从一个小镇搬到了大城市，或者至少在他们看来像是大城市的地方。

00:03:19.930 --> 00:03:48.820
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, a get-together of single farmers who are trying to get other farmers to stay in rural America by marrying them. Act three-- Three Chicago teenagers make the argument that the public housing project that they live in, a high rise building here in one of the biggest cities in the country, is actually a small town. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，一群单身农民的聚会，他们试图通过联姻让其他农民留在美国的农村。第三幕，三名芝加哥青少年提出论点，认为他们居住的公共住房项目——这个国家最大的城市之一里的一栋高层建筑——实际上就是一个小镇。请继续收听。

00:03:48.820 --> 00:04:25.000
<v Ira Glass>Act one-- City Mouse, Country Mouse. One of the classic American stories is the migration from small town to big city, and all the personal growth, and dangers, and freedom that happen when you make that move. Our contributing editor, Sarah Vowell, went through that change yourself, with her family, but with one small catch. The big city that her family moved to wasn't New York, or Los Angeles, or Boston. It was Bozeman, Montana, population 30,000. Which sounds like a small town to anybody who lives in a city, but it was, in fact, 30 times larger than Braggs, Oklahoma where they started.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，“城市老鼠，乡村老鼠”。经典的美国故事之一是从小镇向大城市的迁徙，以及当你做出这种改变时所经历的所有个人成长、危险和自由。我们的特约编辑莎拉·沃威尔和她的家人也经历了这种转变，但有一个小插曲。她家人搬去的大城市不是纽约、洛杉矶或波士顿。而是蒙大拿州的博兹曼，人口3万。对于任何住在城市里的人来说，这听起来像个小镇，但实际上，它比他们出发地俄克拉荷马州的布拉格斯大了30倍。

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:45.980
<v Sarah Vowell>We were hopeful immigrants from small town Oklahoma who set out for a better life in small-town Montana and became new people. Where else but America can an okra-eating, twang-talking, God-fearing, good girl like me be given the opportunity to turn into the liquored-up, opinion-mongering heathen I am today?
<v Sarah Vowell>我们是从俄克拉荷马州小镇出发，怀揣希望的移民，去蒙大拿州的小镇追求更好的生活，并成为了新人。除了美国，哪里还能让像我这样吃秋葵、说话带乡音、敬畏上帝的乖女孩，有机会变成今天这个喝得烂醉、爱发表意见的异教徒呢？

00:04:45.980 --> 00:05:04.710
<v Sarah Vowell>My twin sister and I were born Okies in Muskogee, because that's where the nearest hospital was. But we lived in Braggs, a dusty little Muskogee county nowhere, home to a 1,000 people, four churches, one school, a couple of stores, and a much vilified bar called The Little Oklahoma.
<v Sarah Vowell>我和孪生姐姐是“奥基人”（俄克拉荷马人），出生在马斯科吉，因为那是最近的医院所在地。但我们住在布拉格斯，一个多尘的、马斯科吉县的无名小地，有1000人口、四座教堂、一所学校、几家商店，以及一家臭名昭著的酒吧，叫“小俄克拉荷马”。

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:34.670
<v Sarah Vowell>Our mother took us to Braggs' Pentecostal church three times a week, where I got saved, got baptized, and prayed with her and the others in the ladies prayer meeting for The Little Oklahoma to be shut down. And I sang, sang in church, sang at home, sang along with my transistor radio to the Tulsa country station. I sang for God, because I knew God was listening. When I was six, I got a tape recorder for Christmas. And here's the first song I sang into it.
<v Sarah Vowell>母亲每周带我们去布拉格斯的五旬节派教堂三次，我在那里得救、受洗，并和她以及其他人在妇女祈祷会上祈祷，希望“小俄克拉荷马”酒吧关门。我唱歌，在教堂唱，在家里唱，跟着我的晶体管收音机唱塔尔萨乡村音乐电台的歌。我为上帝而唱，因为我知道上帝在听。六岁时，我收到了圣诞礼物——一台录音机。这是我录下的第一首歌。

00:05:34.670 --> 00:06:01.960
<v Sarah Vowell>[SINGING] I will be a helper at home, at church, at school. I will be a helper, obeying Bible rules. When there's work for me to do, I'll do it happily. I will be a helper to everyone I see.
<v Sarah Vowell>[歌唱] 我要做个好帮手，在家里、在教堂、在学校。我要做个好帮手，遵守圣经的规则。当有工作要做时，我会开心地完成。我要成为我所见之人的帮手。

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:32.030
<v Ira Glass>I hate that song more than any other. Every time it pops into my head, I shiver, because it's a spooky reminder of the docile woman I might have become had I stayed in that town, in that church, where there are so many rules and so many eyes upon you. Not to mention the fact that God himself ran the biggest stake-out of them all, and you knew that, come Judgment Day, you'd account for every last slip your preacher and his minions had somehow missed.
<v Ira Glass>我讨厌那首歌胜过任何其他歌。每当它浮现在脑海中，我就会发抖，因为它时刻提醒着我，如果我留在那个镇上，留在那个有很多规矩、有很多双眼睛盯着你的教堂里，我可能会变成一个多么唯唯诺诺的女人。更不用说上帝本人就在进行着最大规模的监视，你知道，等到审判日，你得为你牧师及其爪牙们漏掉的每一个小过失负责。

00:06:32.030 --> 00:06:57.810
<v Ira Glass>Small towns are always hotbeds of surveillance, but when you add fundamentalist religion to the mix, the things you can't do outnumber the things you can. Sex is bad. Drinking's bad. Smoking's bad. Women standing up to their husbands is bad. Questioning scripture is bad. Not attending church is bad. Cussing is pretty damn bad. And, as my father would soon find out, moving away is really, really bad.
<v Ira Glass>小镇总是监视的温床，但当加上原教旨主义宗教时，你不能做的事比能做的事还多。性是坏的。喝酒是坏的。抽烟是坏的。女人顶撞丈夫是坏的。质疑圣经是坏的。不去教堂是坏的。咒骂是非常糟糕的。而且，正如我父亲很快会发现的，搬走是非常、非常糟糕的。

00:06:57.810 --> 00:07:08.020
<v Ira Glass>If you ask him why we left Braggs and moved to Bozeman-- and believe me, my mother's family has never stopped asking-- he'll launch into this whole song and dance about his health.
<v Ira Glass>如果你问他为什么要离开布拉格斯搬到博兹曼——相信我，我母亲的家人从来没停止过询问——他就会开始长篇大论，把原因归结为他的健康。

00:07:08.020 --> 00:07:15.590
<v Sarah Vowell's Father>One of the main deals was my asthma, health problems.
<v Sarah Vowell's Father>主要原因之一是我的哮喘，健康问题。

00:07:15.590 --> 00:07:19.270
<v Sarah Vowell>Now, Dad, you've been giving us the old, health line for years.
<v Sarah Vowell>爸，那种老掉牙的健康理由你都已经说了好几年了。

00:07:19.270 --> 00:07:25.550
<v Sarah Vowell's Father>Yeah, that was one of the reasons for leaving there.
<v Sarah Vowell's Father>是的，那确实是离开那里的原因之一。

00:07:25.550 --> 00:07:28.550
<v Sarah Vowell>Yeah, what was the real reason?
<v Sarah Vowell>是啊，真正的原因是什么？

00:07:28.550 --> 00:07:32.100
<v Sarah Vowell's Father>The real reason, I wanted out of there.
<v Sarah Vowell's Father>真正的原因，我想离开那里。

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:33.510
<v Sarah Vowell>Why?
<v Sarah Vowell>为什么？

00:07:33.510 --> 00:07:40.120
<v Sarah Vowell's Father>Go somewhere that was a lot cooler, and--
<v Sarah Vowell's Father>去个凉快得多的地方，而且——

00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:46.760
<v Sarah Vowell>And any other reasons? I mean, what, specifically--
<v Sarah Vowell>还有其他原因吗？我是说，具体是什么——

00:07:46.760 --> 00:07:48.660
<v Sarah Vowell's Father>The hunting is real good here.
<v Sarah Vowell's Father>这里的狩猎条件真的很好。

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:53.410
<v Sarah Vowell>Dad, you're not answering the question. Tell me what you hated about Oklahoma.
<v Sarah Vowell>爸，你没回答问题。告诉我你讨厌俄克拉荷马州的什么。

00:07:53.410 --> 00:07:59.130
<v Sarah Vowell's Father>Mainly, kind of the heat in the summertime.
<v Sarah Vowell's Father>主要是，夏天那种热度。

00:07:59.130 --> 00:08:15.720
<v Sarah Vowell>My dad's being diplomatic. No matter how many times I reassured him that this radio program is not on any stations in Oklahoma, and that no one in our family would listen to it if it were, he still didn't want to come right out and say why he wanted to leave Oklahoma.
<v Sarah Vowell>我爸在打外交辞令。无论我多少次向他保证这个广播节目在俄克拉荷马州的任何电台都没有播出，而且即便播出，我们家人也不会听，他还是不想直接说出他想离开俄克拉荷马的原因。

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:39.730
<v Sarah Vowell>It was to get away from family, his and my mother's. They were nosy, and they were everywhere. Every summer, itching to get away from them and Braggs, he'd drive us to the Rockies. Oh, look at the pretty mountains, Mom would say. That Old Faithful sure is something. My mother, my sister, and I just thought we were on vacation. Turns out Dad was scouting out an escape route.
<v Sarah Vowell>是为了躲开家人，他那边的和母亲那边的。他们太爱打听，而且无处不在。每年夏天，为了逃离他们和布拉格斯，他都会开车带我们去落基山脉。看那美丽的群山，妈妈会说。那“老忠实”喷泉真是名不虚传。我母亲、姐姐和我只是以为我们在度假。原来我爸是在探查逃跑路线。

00:08:39.730 --> 00:09:02.790
<v Sarah Vowell>He still wanted to live in a small town. It's just that he wanted to live in a small town where he didn't know anyone. So he picked Bozeman. It was the right size, surrounded by gorgeous mountains, and best of all, had the vital statistics he was after-- Vowell family, 4, acquaintances 0.
<v Sarah Vowell>他仍然想住在小镇。只是他想住在一个人都不认识的小镇。所以他选了博兹曼。它大小合适，四周群山环绕，最棒的是，拥有他追求的统计数据——沃威尔家族，4人，熟人，0。

00:09:02.790 --> 00:09:34.110
<v Sarah Vowell>Arriving in Bozeman at the age of 11, I felt like we had just moved to Paris, a town of culture and ideas, of libraries, and movie theaters, and record stores. I still celebrate the first day we got there, June 5, as a birthday of sorts. Bozeman had miles and miles of cement for us to rollerskate around on, instead of the stunted 50-foot strip of sidewalk in our Braggs backyard. My sister Amy remembers pulling into town.
<v Sarah Vowell>11岁那年到达博兹曼，我觉得我们像是搬到了巴黎，一个充满文化和思想、有图书馆、电影院和唱片店的城镇。我至今仍庆祝我们到达的那天，6月5日，作为某种生日。博兹曼有连绵数英里的水泥地供我们滑旱冰，而不是布拉格斯后院那条发育不良的50英尺长的人行道。我姐姐艾米记得车开进镇子时的情景。

00:09:34.110 --> 00:10:09.610
<v Amy Vowell>I remember coming over the pass, and we'd been driving through the mountains or whatever, and it seemed really, super huge. It was weird being around so much concrete and just organization. We were just set loose out in the world, too. Where, in Oklahoma, we could wander around, but it was always like in the front pasture, or the back woods, or walk over to our pa's house or something. None of this like, just cruise around a whole town. And it kind of freaked us out.
<v Amy Vowell>我记得越过山口，我们一直在山里开车之类的，它看起来真的超级巨大。被这么多混凝土和组织感包围感觉很奇怪。我们就像是被释放到了世界上一样。在俄克拉荷马，我们可以四处闲逛，但总是在前牧场、后树林，或者走到爷爷家之类的。没有那种，在这个城镇里到处乱逛的感觉。那让我们有点惊慌。

00:10:09.610 --> 00:10:11.500
<v Sarah Vowell>But it was exciting, too.
<v Sarah Vowell>但也挺令人兴奋的。

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:12.620
<v Amy Vowell>It was.
<v Amy Vowell>是啊。

00:10:12.620 --> 00:10:50.330
<v Sarah Vowell>One of the things I liked about living in Bozeman was that there was a library. Remember in Braggs, the whole school, wasn't it like a shelf with some books on it for 12 grades of people? And Bozeman had separate buildings that were libraries, and how fun that was to just go and look at all the books we wanted.
<v Sarah Vowell>我在博兹曼住着喜欢的其中一点就是有图书馆。还记得在布拉格斯吗，整个学校，难道不是只有一架子书供12个年级的人用吗？而博兹曼有单独的图书馆大楼，能进去看所有我们想看的书，真是太棒了。

00:10:50.330 --> 00:11:15.250
<v Ira Glass>In Bozeman, I thrived, my sister blossomed, my father found his thrill, but the move was hard on my mom. She missed her epic family. The thing that made my father squirm about small town life, being surrounded by people who know you, was exactly what she'd loved about it. Her family is huge and hilarious, with big mouths and bigger hearts.
<v Ira Glass>在博兹曼，我茁壮成长，姐姐灿烂绽放，父亲找到了他的兴奋点，但这搬迁对我妈来说很难。她想念她那庞大的家族。当初让我父亲对小镇生活感到不安的那件事——被认识你的人包围——正是她所热爱的。她的家族庞大又风趣，个个心直口快，却有着更宽广的心胸。

00:11:15.250 --> 00:12:00.290
<v Ira Glass>And up north, she missed her fiery, southern-style church. There weren't any other Pentecostals in Bozeman, so we ended up at the most frigid, watered-down, nondenominational Protestant house of worship possible. We were used to this wrathful, angry, Old Testament Creator. And sitting through week after week of God is love got pretty bland. And after a few years, our church visits tapered off, which was fine by me. Mom, on the other hand, had to deal with not only her own loss of spiritual guidance and community, she had to sit by and watch her children losing Christian steam.
<v Ira Glass>在北方，她怀念她那热情的南方风格教堂。博兹曼没有其他五旬节派教徒，所以我们最终去了当地最冷淡、最稀释、无教派的新教礼拜堂。我们习惯了那个愤怒的、旧约时代的造物主。每周坐在那里听着“上帝就是爱”，感觉很平淡。几年后，我们去教堂的次数就减少了，这正合我意。而妈妈，不仅要应对她失去精神指引和社区的痛苦，还得坐在一旁看着她的孩子们失去基督徒的热情。

00:12:00.290 --> 00:12:04.240
<v Sarah Vowell>Well, let's talk about something we generally avoid talking about.
<v Sarah Vowell>好吧，我们来谈谈一个我们通常会避免谈论的话题。

00:12:04.240 --> 00:12:06.410
<v Sarah Vowell's Mother>OK.
<v Sarah Vowell's Mother>好的。

00:12:06.410 --> 00:12:29.470
<v Sarah Vowell>Do you think-- Well, as you know, at some point, I basically lost my faith and never got it back. Is that a regret of yours? Do you think if we had stayed in Oklahoma, I would still be right there by your side at church with you?
<v Sarah Vowell>你觉得——嗯，如你所知，在某个时候，我基本上失去了信仰，就再也没找回来。这是一个遗憾吗？你觉得如果我们留在俄克拉荷马，我会不会还在教堂里和你站在一起？

00:12:29.470 --> 00:13:09.060
<v Sarah Vowell's Mother>That's interesting, Sarah, because I do think about that a lot. I used to just really think, especially when I found out that you had lost your faith, I thought, if we would have stayed in Oklahoma, this wouldn't have happened to Sarah. I feel really guilty and really bad that we moved to Montana, and Sarah has lost this. But over the years, as I've seen you grow up and realize and know what kind of person you are, I'm wondering, even in Oklahoma, would you have began to search in different areas and maybe doubt?
<v Sarah Vowell's Mother>这很有趣，莎拉，因为我确实经常想那个问题。我过去真的觉得，尤其是当我发现你失去了信仰时，我想，如果我们留在俄克拉荷马，莎拉就不会这样了。我们搬到蒙大拿州，莎拉失去了这一切，我感到非常内疚，非常难过。但多年来，看着你长大，了解到你是个什么样的人，我在想，即便在俄克拉荷马，你是否也会开始在不同领域探索，甚至产生怀疑？

00:13:09.060 --> 00:13:10.910
<v Sarah Vowell>Are you calling me a bad seed, Mom?
<v Sarah Vowell>妈，你是在说我是个坏苗子吗？

00:13:10.910 --> 00:13:36.240
<v Sarah Vowell's Mother>No, no. I'm not. No, I'm not. I'm not. I love you very much, and I'm very proud of the person you are, and that's your choice. But it don't keep me from praying every night that you'll return to your faith, Sarah.
<v Sarah Vowell's Mother>不，不。我不是。不，我不是。我非常爱你，我为你成为今天这样的人感到非常骄傲，那是你的选择。但这并不能阻止我每天晚上祈祷你能回归信仰，莎拉。

00:13:36.240 --> 00:14:01.550
<v Sarah Vowell>Amy told me-- I don't remember this-- but Amy said that once, when we were about 13 or so, when we were in Bozeman, you sat us down, and you asked us if we wanted to go back to Braggs. And I would assume the reason for that is, that if we would have said yes, because you obviously wanted to go back, then we would have ganged up on dad or something. Do you remember that?
<v Sarah Vowell>艾米告诉我——我不记得了——但艾米说，大约我们13岁左右，我们在博兹曼的时候，你曾把我们坐下来，问我们想不想回布拉格斯。我想原因应该是，如果我们答应了，因为你显然想回去，那么我们就会联合起来对付爸爸之类的。你记得吗？

00:14:01.550 --> 00:14:04.030
<v Sarah Vowell's Mother>Yes, I do remember that.
<v Sarah Vowell's Mother>是的，我记得。

00:14:04.030 --> 00:14:07.800
<v Sarah Vowell>Were you really? How was it for you when we said we didn't want to go back?
<v Sarah Vowell>你们当时真的是那么想的吗？当我们说不想回去时，你是什么感觉？

00:14:07.800 --> 00:14:48.240
<v Sarah Vowell's Mother>Oh, I was totally devastated. Yes, I remember that very well. I think you were pretty quiet. And it was really neat what Amy said to me. She said, Momma, we understand that you're very unhappy here, and that you miss home, and if you feel that you need to go back, that's OK with Sarah and I. But we want to stay here. And I looked at you, and I said, do you want to stay here, Sarah? And you said, yes, I do.
<v Sarah Vowell's Mother>噢，我当时彻底崩溃了。是的，我记得很清楚。我觉得你当时挺安静的。艾米对我说的话真的很棒。她说，妈妈，我们理解你在那儿很不开心，你想念家乡，如果你觉得你需要回去，我和莎拉没意见。但我们想留在这里。我看着你，说，莎拉，你想留在这里吗？你说，是的，我想。

00:14:48.240 --> 00:15:14.530
<v Sarah Vowell's Mother>And so, I mean, I immediately wanted to burst into tears, but I thought, I need to compose myself here. And later that night, as I thought about it, I guess it was a real turning point for me, actually, to start being happy here in Bozeman. Because I thought, OK, I can't leave my children, so I better get it together.
<v Sarah Vowell's Mother>所以，我是说，我当时立刻想大哭一场，但我心想，我得保持冷静。那天晚上晚些时候，当我想到这一点时，我想，那确实是我开始在博兹曼快乐生活的转折点。因为我想，好吧，我不能离开我的孩子们，所以我最好调整好状态。

00:15:14.530 --> 00:15:29.550
<v Sarah Vowell's Mother>Because-- and I know you really don't like to hear me say this, Sarah-- because you and Amy have been and are the most important thing in my life. And so there's no way I could have ever gone away and left you.
<v Sarah Vowell's Mother>因为——我知道你真的不喜欢听我这么说，莎拉——因为你和艾米一直是我生命中最重要的人。所以我绝不可能独自离开，留下你们。

00:15:29.550 --> 00:16:03.570
<v Sarah Vowell>There's so much mother-daughter history in the way my Mom says, I know you don't like hearing me say this, because she knows I don't like hearing her say that I'm the most important thing in her life sometimes because of the guilt that involves. And before I started working on this story, I'd always felt badly about the way she sacrificed her happiness for mine. I grew up believing she would've been better off in her little Oklahoma hometown, surrounded by family, embraced by the church. But I found out I was wrong.
<v Sarah Vowell>我妈说“我知道你不喜欢听我这么说”的方式里，藏着太多的母女过往，因为她知道，有时我不喜欢听她说我依然是她生命中最重要的人，因为这其中包含了负罪感。在开始做这个故事之前，我一直对她为了我牺牲自己的幸福感到愧疚。我长大后一直认为，如果她留在她那个俄克拉荷马的小家乡，被家人包围，被教堂拥抱，她会过得更好。但我发现我错了。

00:16:03.570 --> 00:16:07.120
<v Sarah Vowell>Now, do you still want to go back to Oklahoma?
<v Sarah Vowell>现在，你还想回俄克拉荷马吗？

00:16:07.120 --> 00:16:11.090
<v Sarah Vowell's Mother>Oh, no. No, I could never live in Oklahoma again.
<v Sarah Vowell's Mother>噢，不。不，我再也无法住在俄克拉荷马了。

00:16:11.090 --> 00:16:12.450
<v Sarah Vowell>Why not?
<v Sarah Vowell>为什么不呢？

00:16:12.450 --> 00:16:43.850
<v Sarah Vowell's Mother>I really think that I've changed too much, Sarah. I mean, I love my brothers back there, dearly, and my best friend, Cathy, and there's lots of wonderful people that I just adore and love so much back there, but I do feel that I've changed so much in the 15 years that we've lived here that I could not live in Oklahoma again.
<v Sarah Vowell's Mother>我真的觉得我改变太多了，莎拉。我是说，我深爱着在那里的兄弟们，还有我最好的朋友凯茜，那里有许多我很崇拜、很爱的人，但我真的觉得在这15年的生活里，我改变了太多，以至于我无法再住在俄克拉荷马。

00:16:43.850 --> 00:16:49.080
<v Sarah Vowell>So now, do you think then that Braggs is just too small?
<v Sarah Vowell>所以现在，你觉得布拉格斯太小了吗？

00:16:49.080 --> 00:16:51.790
<v Sarah Vowell's Mother>Yes, I do.
<v Sarah Vowell's Mother>是的。

00:16:51.790 --> 00:17:00.030
<v Sarah Vowell>And when you were growing up there, and when you were living your whole life there, before you moved to Montana, would you ever imagine that that would be true for you?
<v Sarah Vowell>当你还在那儿长大，当你一辈子都住在那里，在你搬到蒙大拿之前，你曾想象过会有这么一天吗？

00:17:00.030 --> 00:17:16.160
<v Sarah Vowell's Mother>No, no. Growing up there, I never even dreamed in my wildest dreams about moving away from there. I just always thought, I'll just meet someone here, and get married, and live the rest of my life here.
<v Sarah Vowell's Mother>不，不。在那里长大时，我甚至从未做梦想到过要搬离那里。我一直想，我就在那儿遇个人，结婚，在那里过完余生。

00:17:16.160 --> 00:17:17.100
<v Sarah Vowell>Really?
<v Sarah Vowell>真的吗？

00:17:17.100 --> 00:17:19.450
<v Sarah Vowell's Mother>Yes.
<v Sarah Vowell's Mother>是的。

00:17:19.450 --> 00:17:25.140
<v Sarah Vowell>I guess your dream didn't come true. Is that OK with you?
<v Sarah Vowell>我想你的梦没有实现。这没关系吗？

00:17:25.140 --> 00:17:52.650
<v Sarah Vowell's Mother>Oh yeah, yeah. As difficult as it was moving to Bozeman the first few years and all the changes, it really, for all of us, was a good move.
<v Sarah Vowell's Mother>噢，当然，没关系。尽管搬到博兹曼的前几年很困难，经历了所有改变，但这对我们所有人来说，确实是个不错的举动。

00:17:52.650 --> 00:18:10.410
<v Ira Glass>Sarah Vowell's move to big-city Bozeman was so successful that she decided to repeat the experience. She move to Portland, population 500,000, Washington DC, population 600,000, San Francisco, population 700,000, and then, finally, Chicago, population 2.8 million.
<v Ira Glass>莎拉·沃威尔搬到大城市博兹曼非常成功，以至于她决定重复这种经历。她搬到了人口50万的波特兰，人口60万的华盛顿特区，人口70万的旧金山，最后是人口280万的芝加哥。

00:18:10.410 --> 00:18:53.140
<v Ira Glass>Act Two. How do you keep them going down on the farm? Marry 'em. All across America, people are moving away from small towns and rural areas in steady streams. So if you have stayed in a place where there aren't many people, and the ones that are there you've know since birth, how are you going to find somebody to marry? Well, you might join Singles In Agriculture. They hold get-togethers for singles all over the country. Reporter Liz Weil went with This American Life producer Alix Spiegel to a recent get-together in Galveston, Texas, at the Flagship Hotel on the beach, jutting out onto the water. There were about 250 people from 26 states at meals and meetings, day trips, and dances.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕。怎么让他们留在农场？嫁给他们。在美国各地，人们正源源不断地搬离小镇和农村地区。所以如果你留在一个人口稀少的地方，而且那里的人你从小就认识，你要怎么找到结婚对象呢？好吧，你可能会加入“农业单身族”（SIA）。他们在全国各地为单身人士举行聚会。记者利兹·威尔和《美国生活》制片人阿利克斯·斯皮格尔一起参加了最近在德克萨斯州加尔维斯顿海滩上一家伸进水里的旗舰酒店举办的聚会。有来自26个州的约250人参加了聚会、会议、日游和舞会。

00:18:53.140 --> 00:19:14.840
<v Liz Weil>Jim's an extreme case-- smart, charming, 52, looks 40, living on a farm outside Iona, South Dakota, population four. It's 30 miles to the nearest grocery store. The weather is so punishing, cows freeze solid standing up. His dilemma, nobody wants to move to his ranch, and he doesn't want to leave.
<v Liz Weil>吉姆是一个极端的例子——聪明、迷人，52岁，看着像40岁，住在南达科他州爱奥纳外面的农场，人口4人。距离最近的杂货店有30英里远。天气恶劣到牛都会站着冻僵。他的两难境地是，没人愿意搬到他的牧场，而他也不想离开。

00:19:14.840 --> 00:19:26.480
<v Jim>This is not bragging on myself or anything, but I have had a girl offer to buy me a farm in another state if I would move and marry her, but I just can't leave it.
<v Jim>这不是吹嘘自己，但曾有女孩提出，如果我肯搬过去并嫁给我，她愿意为我在其他州买个农场，但我就是离不开这里。

00:19:26.480 --> 00:19:29.060
<v Liz Weil>Was that a hard choice?
<v Liz Weil>那是很难的选择吗？

00:19:29.060 --> 00:19:43.980
<v Jim>Yes, yes it was. I couldn't see why, if I meant that much to her, why she couldn't move there to my place and stuff. And yes, her land had been in her family for a long time, I guess it's--
<v Jim>是，是的。我不明白，如果我对她那么重要，为什么她不能搬到我这里来。而且是的，她的土地在她们家已经传承很久了，我想那是——

00:19:43.980 --> 00:20:03.700
<v Liz Weil>This is a typical problem for people in SIA. They're all farmers, all tied to their land, though Jim's situation is particularly harsh. He tells me he realized that a few years ago when the group's director went out to visit him. Even she was stunned by how isolated he was. She said it would take a remarkable woman to move to his ranch.
<v Liz Weil>这是SIA成员的典型问题。他们都是农民，都与土地紧密相连，尽管吉姆的情况特别严峻。他告诉我，几年前当该组织的负责人去探望他时，他才意识到这点。连她都对他竟然如此孤立感到震惊。她说，得是一个非凡的女性才愿意搬到他的牧场。

00:20:03.700 --> 00:20:08.520
<v Liz Weil>So do  you, realistically, think that you're going to get married?
<v Liz Weil>那么你现实地认为你会结婚吗？

00:20:08.520 --> 00:20:33.220
<v Jim>I would like to. I guess it's always in the back of your mind, you would hope to. But you want an honest answer. I don't think it's going to happen. It's going to take a special girl. You're a city girl, both of you are. I'd ask you, would you be willing to live out, even give the consideration of living out, and trying something like that?
<v Jim>我想的。我想这总是在你的脑后，你会希望如此。但你想要诚实的回答。我不认为会发生。那需要一个特别的女孩。你们是城市女孩，两位都是。我问你们，你们愿意住在那儿，甚至考虑住在那儿，尝试那样的生活吗？

00:20:33.220 --> 00:21:03.820
<v Liz Weil>Jim's own mother even admitted that, had she known where she'd been moving, she might not have married his father and moved to the land. It's Thursday night, the first night of the get-together, and we're all at a dance in a Victorian gazebo, with wood floors so old, women have been asked to wear boots, not heels. Everyone's so happy to be here. I have the sense I'm talking to people who don't get to talk to other human beings enough.
<v Liz Weil>吉姆的母亲甚至承认，如果她早知道自己要搬到哪儿，她可能就不会嫁给他父亲并搬到那块土地上。这是周四晚上，聚会的第一个晚上，我们都在一个维多利亚式的凉亭里跳舞，木地板太老了，女士们被要求穿靴子，不能穿高跟鞋。每个人都为能来到这里而感到高兴。我觉得我在和那些没机会与其他人类充分交流的人交谈。

00:21:03.820 --> 00:21:17.410
<v Liz Weil>About 10:00, I sit down next to Martha, a twice-widowed woman from Indiana. She's small and nearly 60, wearing a sweatshirt she has decorated with glitter and beads. SIA is the focus of her life.
<v Liz Weil>大约10点，我坐在玛莎旁边，一位来自印第安纳州的两次丧偶女性。她个子娇小，快60岁了，穿着一件用亮片和珠子装饰的运动衫。SIA是她生活的中心。

00:21:17.410 --> 00:21:55.340
<v Martha>It gives me something to live for, because I'm very lonely, and I need this to keep me going. Because the first camp-out I went to, I cried when it was time to go home. And they was singing church music, and I couldn't sing, I was crying so hard. And I was giving everybody hugs. I love hugs. Then this one guy just held me, and held me. He said, are you OK? He just held me, and held me till I got calmed down. I said, it just shows how lonely I am, I guess.
<v Martha>它给了我活下去的动力，因为我非常孤独，我需要这个来支持我。因为我参加的第一次露营，回家的时候我哭了。他们唱着教堂的歌，我唱不出来，因为哭得太厉害了。我在给每个人拥抱。我喜欢拥抱。然后一个男人只是抱着我，抱着我。他说，你还好吗？他只是抱着我，一直抱着我，直到我平静下来。我说，我想这正显示了我有多孤独。

00:21:55.340 --> 00:22:27.390
<v Liz Weil>Martha tells me that her favorite dance is The Waterfall. How it works is that the women form one line, the men form another, and the two meet in the middle like a zipper, couples dancing up the floor. Martha tells me she likes The Waterfall because you don't need a partner. You just stand in line, and the line moves. You pull a body close, get hugged.
<v Liz Weil>玛莎告诉我，她最喜欢的舞是“瀑布”。它是这样运作的，女士们排成一行，男士们排成另一行，两人像拉链一样在中间汇合，伴侣舞动着穿过舞池。玛莎告诉我她喜欢“瀑布”是因为你不需要固定舞伴。你只需要排队，队伍就会移动。你把一个人拉近，得到一个拥抱。

00:22:27.390 --> 00:22:56.060
<v Liz Weil>The next day is Friday. Half the farmers board yellow buses for the NASA Space Center. The other half trek to Houston for a World's Fair-style livestock show. About every 15 minutes, over the course of the day, somebody tries to convince me, no, this group isn't about hooking up, it's about friendship, camaraderie, everyone's one big family. But later in the afternoon, I bump into a group of SIA women who are clearly on the make. One's just bought a t-shirt that reads, cowboy butts drive me nuts.
<v Liz Weil>第二天是周五。一半的农民坐上黄色校车去NASA航天中心。另一半人去休斯顿参加世博会风格的牲畜展。在这一天里，每隔15分钟左右，就会有人试图说服我，不，这个团体不是为了猎艳，是为了友谊、同志情谊，每个人都是一个大家庭。但那天下午晚些时候，我撞见了一群SIA的女性，她们显然是冲着找对象去的。一个刚买了一件T恤，上面写着“牛仔裤里的屁股让我疯狂”。

00:22:56.060 --> 00:22:58.570
<v Sia Woman 1>Maroon shirt ladies, maroon shirt, to the left.
<v Sia Woman 1>穿栗色衬衫的女士们，栗色衬衫，到左边来。

00:22:58.570 --> 00:23:03.190
<v Kate>Oh, when we were eating, oh, jeez did they walk past us.
<v Kate>噢，当我们吃饭的时候，天哪，他们走过我们身边。

00:23:03.190 --> 00:23:07.960
<v Sia Woman 3>No, no, the best one was the guy that was showing the palomino horse. He was good.
<v Sia Woman 3>不，不，最好的是那个展示帕洛米诺马的家伙。他很不错。

00:23:07.960 --> 00:23:11.300
<v Sia Woman 4>I'm looking at the guy in the black hat right here.
<v Sia Woman 4>我正盯着这边戴黑帽子的家伙看。

00:23:11.300 --> 00:23:15.940
<v Liz Weil>So what do you look for in the jeans, Lee's, Levi's, Wrangler's?
<v Liz Weil>那你喜欢什么样的牛仔裤，Lee牌、Levi's、Wrangler's？

00:23:15.940 --> 00:23:17.420
<v Kate>What's in them.
<v Kate>我喜欢穿在里面的那个人。

00:23:17.420 --> 00:23:42.850
<v Liz Weil>These  girls are a rowdy, hardcore bunch. They're youngish, around 30, loud, constantly making cracks. Kate, the most vocal of them, is broad and strong, a dairy cow wrangler from Wisconsin. In this crowd, she plays the tomboy. She brags about telling the men she dances with that if someone wants to pick a fight, she'll do the punching. But once alone in her hotel room, her tough girl attitude fades away.
<v Liz Weil>这些女孩是一群吵闹的硬核派。她们正年轻，大约30岁，声音洪亮，不断地开玩笑。凯特，她们中最爱说话的一个，身材魁梧，是来自威斯康星州的奶牛牧人。在这个人群中，她扮演假小子的角色。她吹嘘说她会告诉和她跳舞的男人，如果有人想挑衅，她会挥拳回击。但一旦独自待在酒店房间里，她女汉子的姿态就会消失。

00:23:42.850 --> 00:23:52.940
<v Kate>I think of myself as a lady. If you treat me like a lady, I mean, I treat them well. I guess, I'm more traditional, older-fashioned, I guess they call it.
<v Kate>我觉得自己是个淑女。如果你把我当淑女对待，我就也会对他好。我想，我更传统，更老派，我想他们是这么称呼的。

00:23:52.940 --> 00:23:59.320
<v Liz Weil>She goes on to tell me about a friend of hers, a woman who prayed every night to meet somebody and eventually did.
<v Liz Weil>她接着告诉我关于她一个朋友的事，一个每晚祈祷能遇到某人并最终如愿以偿的女性。

00:23:59.320 --> 00:24:02.820
<v Liz Weil>So do you do that? Do you pray about what the kind of person--?
<v Liz Weil>那你也那样做吗？你会为你要遇到什么样的人祈祷吗——？

00:24:02.820 --> 00:24:28.080
<v Kate>What? Well, yeah. I mean, you pray that God leads you or guides you to, or guides him and you to meet sometime, and what kind of guy you would like. And she told me, be specific. You can't just say a guy that's got two legs, a hat, and whatever, a belt buckle.
<v Kate>什么？嗯，是的。我是说，你会祈祷上帝引领或指导你，或者指导他和你会面，以及你想要什么样的男人。她告诉我，要具体。你不能只说一个有两条腿、一顶帽子、腰带扣的男人。

00:24:28.080 --> 00:24:30.670
<v Liz Weil>So are you following her directions? Are you being specific?
<v Liz Weil>那你遵循她的指示了吗？你有具体化吗？

00:24:30.670 --> 00:24:34.930
<v Kate>No, I mean, I do this, like, every night.
<v Kate>没有，我是说，我每天晚上都这样做。

00:24:34.930 --> 00:24:36.890
<v Ernie>This going to smell terrible.
<v Ernie>这闻起来太糟糕了。

00:24:36.890 --> 00:24:39.110
<v Sally>Oh, it does.
<v Sally>噢，确实。

00:24:39.110 --> 00:25:06.630
<v Liz Weil>Down the hall from Kate are Ernie and Sally, a couple who met through SIA. He's 50ish, from Oklahoma, grows sesame and delivers mail. She's a school teacher from Kansas, incredibly hot, in a short, tight dress and baby-doll tee. At this point in their relationship, they only book one hotel room. And right now, Ernie is in there fixing Sally's boot. The smell of epoxy is mind-numbing. They detail their first date.
<v Liz Weil>在凯特的走廊对面是厄尼和萨莉，一对通过SIA认识的夫妇。他50出头，来自俄克拉荷马州，种植芝麻并担任邮递员。她是一名来自堪萨斯的学校老师，非常火辣，穿着短而紧的连衣裙和娃娃衫。此时他们的关系中，他们只订了一个酒店房间。现在，厄尼正在里面修理萨莉的靴子。环氧树脂的味道令人麻木。他们详细描述了他们的第一次约会。

00:25:06.630 --> 00:25:15.430
<v Ernie>We were going to a dance, Halloween dance, and I got to her house.
<v Ernie>我们当时要去跳舞，万圣节舞会，我到了她家。

00:25:15.430 --> 00:25:18.190
<v Sally>Driving six hours to get there.
<v Sally>开车六个小时才到那儿。

00:25:18.190 --> 00:25:24.270
<v Ernie>Drove six hours to get there, and in less then 15 minutes, she was begging me to take her dress off.
<v Ernie>开了六小时车，不到15分钟，她就在求我脱掉她的裙子了。

00:25:24.270 --> 00:25:33.520
<v Liz Weil>OK, let me explain. Ernie had brought over a blue flapper dress. His former wife had made it. Sally wanted to look at it, maybe wear it as a costume.
<v Liz Weil>好啦，让我解释一下。厄尼带来了一件蓝色拍拍舞裙。他前妻做的。萨莉想看看，也许可以当作戏服穿。

00:25:33.520 --> 00:25:44.510
<v Ernie>I get there, and she's, oh, I got to try that dress on. She looked at it. She ran in the bathroom and put this dress on. And I waited, and I waited, and pretty soon I heard this, Ernie.
<v Ernie>我到了那儿，她说，哦，我得试试那条裙子。她看了看。她跑进浴室把裙子穿上了。我等啊等，很快我听到这个，厄尼。

00:25:44.510 --> 00:25:46.240
<v Sally>Well, I put the dress on.
<v Sally>好吧，我穿上那件裙子了。

00:25:46.240 --> 00:25:47.680
<v Ernie>Help me.
<v Ernie>帮帮我。

00:25:47.680 --> 00:25:53.240
<v Sally>And it was tight. It was really tight. So we kind of stand there and talk awhile, and I'm just thinking--
<v Sally>而且它很紧。真的很紧。所以我们就在那儿站着聊了一会儿，我只是在想——

00:25:53.240 --> 00:25:55.810
<v Ernie>And I said, well, what do I do? And I was, where do you grab?
<v Ernie>然后我说，好吧，我该怎么做？我就问，你往哪抓？

00:25:55.810 --> 00:26:00.220
<v Sally>He goes, where do you want me to get a hold of you?
<v Sally>他回答说，你想让我往哪儿抓？

00:26:00.220 --> 00:26:18.450
<v Liz Weil>Both Ernie and Sally lost their spouses. They both felt sparks five or six months ago when they first met. They live a reasonable distance apart by SIA standards, but, and this is the problem with a national organization of rural singles, the logistics make it nearly impossible to date.
<v Liz Weil>厄尼和萨莉都失去了配偶。五六个月前他们第一次见面时都感到火花四溅。按照SIA的标准，他们住得距离适中，但——这是全国农村单身组织的问题——后勤使得约会几乎不可能。

00:26:18.450 --> 00:26:23.280
<v Sally>404 miles from my door to his door.
<v Sally>从我家门口到他家门口有404英里。

00:26:23.280 --> 00:26:25.240
<v Liz Weil>And how often do you get a chance to see each other?
<v Liz Weil>你们多久有机会见一次面？

00:26:25.240 --> 00:26:43.110
<v Sally>About every two weeks we get together, every couple weeks. It's a good six-hour drive. I don't care how you go. Phone bills are pretty high, too. Be a lot cheaper if you married me, wouldn't it? He says I push him a little bit. Can you believe that?
<v Sally>大约每两周聚一次。开车至少要六个小时，无论你怎么走。电话费也很高。嫁给我会便宜得多，不是吗？他说我有点逼他。你能相信吗？

00:26:43.110 --> 00:26:46.170
<v Liz Weil>I can't believe that. Do you feel like she pushes you a little bit?
<v Liz Weil>我不敢相信。你觉得她逼你了吗？

00:26:46.170 --> 00:26:51.200
<v Ernie>No, not a little bit, a whole lot.
<v Ernie>不，不是一点点，是逼得很紧。

00:26:51.200 --> 00:27:05.510
<v Liz Weil>Sally and Ernie have this little routine. Like most set pieces couples have, it's only half in jest. She ribs him constantly about getting married, he hedges. They love each other, but they're at different points in their lives.
<v Liz Weil>萨莉和厄尼有这个小套路。像大多数固定伴侣那样，这只是一半开玩笑。她不断地拿结婚的事取笑他，他则回避。他们爱着对方，但他们处于生命中不同的阶段。

00:27:05.510 --> 00:27:07.830
<v Ernie>How long you been single?
<v Ernie>你单身多久了？

00:27:07.830 --> 00:27:12.620
<v Sally>11 years. Husband died, had a heart attack.
<v Sally>11年了。丈夫去世了，心脏病发作。

00:27:12.620 --> 00:27:27.710
<v Ernie>And I've been a year and eight months. So I've got about a year before I might decide whether I'm going to do anything and who with.
<v Ernie>我单身了一年八个月。所以我还有大约一年时间来决定我是否要做什么，以及和谁一起。

00:27:27.710 --> 00:27:43.610
<v Liz Weil>Ernie didn't expect to meet somebody so quickly. In fact, he wasn't sure he'd meet somebody at all. He married young, didn't have many girlfriends before that. Right now, this is his first round of dating. It's a heady experience, and one he's not ready to give up yet.
<v Liz Weil>厄尼没想到会这么快遇到某人。事实上，他不确定自己是否会遇到任何人。他结婚很早，之前没交过几个女朋友。现在，这是他的第一轮约会。这是一种令人兴奋的经历，他还没准备好放弃。

00:27:43.610 --> 00:28:02.010
<v Ernie>Women knew, if you're widowed, they're interested. It's definitely a plus over someone that's divorced.
<v Ernie>女人们知道，如果你是鳏夫，她们很感兴趣。这绝对比离婚的要有优势。

00:28:02.010 --> 00:28:28.420
<v Liz Weil>Friday night is the second dance. And when I bump into Ernie, he's passing out these business cards. They have rainbows on them, and they read, Ernest Ernie W. Schmidt, married 32 years, widowed, I'm attracted to you, let's be good friends. Sally, as you might imagine, is not so thrilled about this. She has enough good friends, at least for the time being. While Ernie mingles, we talk to her in the back room.
<v Liz Weil>周五晚上是第二次舞会。当我撞见厄尼时，他正在分发这些名片。上面有彩虹，写着：“欧内斯特·厄尼·W·施密特，已婚32年，丧偶，我被你吸引了，让我们做个好朋友吧”。萨莉，你可以想象，对此并不怎么兴奋。至少暂时来说，她有足够的好朋友了。当厄尼去社交时，我们在后台房间和她谈话。

00:28:28.420 --> 00:28:44.780
<v Sally>Oh, I would marry him. I mean, if we break up, I'm going to just be heartbroken. It's going to be really bad, because my husband's been gone for 11 years, and I haven't met anybody that I would marry but Ernie.
<v Sally>噢，我会嫁给他。我是说，如果我们分手了，我会心碎的。那会很糟糕，因为我丈夫已经离开11年了，除了厄尼，我还没见过任何我想嫁的人。

00:28:44.780 --> 00:29:22.240
<v Liz Weil>Sally stops her interview, saying she better get back to Ernie. She's worried that he'll miss her. A moment later, we head out from the back room. I see Ernie with a blond woman, two-stepping on the dance floor. Half an hour later, we run into Ernie and Sally kissing on the veranda. Sometime after this, Sally sends me a note. On the envelope she writes, in all capitals, I WILL MARRY ERNIE SOMEDAY.
<v Liz Weil>萨莉停止了采访，说她最好回到厄尼身边。她担心他会想念她。片刻之后，我们从后台走出。我看到厄尼和一个金发女人在舞池里跳舞。半小时后，我们在阳台上碰见厄尼和萨莉在亲吻。在那之后不久，萨莉寄给我一张便条。在信封上，她用全大写字母写道：我总有一天会嫁给厄尼。

00:29:22.240 --> 00:29:48.230
<v Liz Weil>The night starts winding down, at least I think it starts winding down until I realize something, nobody's planning on going to sleep. The people who come to SIA spend a lot of time alone. They crave company, affection, basic human contact. It's past midnight when the farmers leave the gazebo and return to the Flagship Hotel. Everybody's tired, but they don't go to bed. The hit the beach.
<v Liz Weil>夜晚开始结束，至少我觉得它开始结束了，直到我意识到一件事：没人打算睡觉。来到SIA的人独自度过了很多时间。他们渴望陪伴、关怀和基本的人类接触。午夜已过，农民们离开凉亭回到旗舰酒店。每个人都很累，但他们没有去睡觉。他们去了海滩。

00:29:48.230 --> 00:29:51.590
<v Jim>Come on, I got to show you something out there.
<v Jim>来吧，我得给你看点东西。

00:29:51.590 --> 00:29:53.130
<v Sia Woman 2>Just remember, I'm a very strong girl.
<v Sia Woman 2>记住，我是个非常强壮的女孩。

00:29:53.130 --> 00:29:58.740
<v Liz Weil>Jim, the first guy I talked to back at the first dance, drags a fully clothed women into the water.
<v Liz Weil>吉姆，那个我在第一次舞会谈到的第一个家伙，把一个穿着全套衣服的女人拖进了水里。

00:29:58.740 --> 00:30:01.700
<v Sia Woman 5>Ah, you're crazy. Is it cold?
<v Sia Woman 5>啊，你疯了。冷吗？

00:30:01.700 --> 00:30:07.440
<v Sia Woman 5>[SQUEALING AND PLAY  SOUNDS FROM THE  SURF]
<v Sia Woman 5>[海浪声中的尖叫和玩耍声]

00:30:07.440 --> 00:30:22.740
<v Liz Weil>There's something really high school-esque about the whole SIA experience. Like right now, the moon is out, the air is salty, and we're in this huge awkward group. And what do people do to cash in on the moment? Pair off? Nope.
<v Liz Weil>整个SIA的经历有一种非常高中时代的感觉。就像现在，月亮出来了，空气里咸咸的，我们处于这群尴尬的人群中。人们会做什么来利用那一刻？结对？不。

00:30:22.740 --> 00:30:25.800
<v Sia Group>[SINGING] Germs, germs, go away--
<v Sia Group>[歌唱] 病菌，病菌，走开——

00:30:25.800 --> 00:30:26.990
<v Liz Weil>They sing.
<v Liz Weil>他们唱歌。

00:30:26.990 --> 00:30:35.330
<v Sia Group>--never, never come to play. I call you ick, you make me sick.
<v Sia Group>——永远，永远别来找我玩。我叫你讨厌，你让我生病。

00:30:35.330 --> 00:30:38.660
<v Liz Weil>Note, none of them has had a drop to drink.
<v Liz Weil>注，他们中没人喝过一滴酒。

00:30:38.660 --> 00:30:46.000
<v Liz Weil>[GENERAL APPLAUSE, HOOTING]
<v Liz Weil>[热烈掌声、欢呼声]

00:30:46.000 --> 00:31:54.410
<v Ira Glass>Evening winds down, and the couples at SIA head toward the climactic night of their weekend, in a minute, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>夜晚接近尾声，SIA的伴侣们正走向他们周末的高潮之夜，稍后，当我们的节目继续。

00:31:54.410 --> 00:32:11.070
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Each week we choose a theme, of course, and invite a variety of writers, and reporters, and documentary producers to take a whack at that theme. Today's theme, Small Towns. Liz Weil's story continues about a four-day get together of SIA, Singles In Agriculture.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周我们都会选择一个主题，并邀请各种作家、记者和纪录片制作人来探讨该主题。今天的主题，“小镇”。利兹·威尔关于SIA——“农业单身族”四天聚会的故事继续。

00:32:11.070 --> 00:32:23.330
<v Liz Weil>Saturday, we wake up to some incredibly tedious meetings, which, by some heroic faith, people manage to love. It completely undermines all critical standards of what's compelling, what's fun.
<v Liz Weil>周六，我们醒来参加一些极其枯燥的会议，凭着某种英雄般的信念，人们竟然乐在其中。这彻底颠覆了关于什么是吸引人、什么是乐趣的所有批判标准。

00:32:23.330 --> 00:32:31.720
<v Sia Speaker 1>Event financial record be forwarded to the national office within 90 days of the last day of the activity.
<v Sia Speaker 1>活动财务记录应在活动结束后的90天内转发给国家办公室。

00:32:31.720 --> 00:32:53.910
<v Liz Weil>Later Saturday afternoon, I catch up with Martha, the woman who told me that SIA gives her reason to live. She's in her room, sewing the final beads on a sweater she's making for tonight's banquet and flag ceremony. The handiwork seems to relax her. She's got this other sweatshirt, her hug sweatshirt, that she wears at the end of every SIA retreat.
<v Liz Weil>周六下午晚些时候，我追上了玛莎，那个告诉我SIA给了她活下去理由的女人。她在房间里，为今晚的宴会和旗帜仪式缝制一件毛衣上的最后一颗珠子。这手工活似乎让她放松了下来。她还有另一件运动衫，她的“拥抱运动衫”，每次SIA撤退时她都会穿。

00:32:53.910 --> 00:33:30.750
<v Martha>I met this friend, and she sent me this bookmark about why God made hugs. And I don't remember it, but I had my daughter enlarge it on the computer and put it on a scroll. And I put it on the sweatshirt. And I tell people that I didn't invent the hugs, God did, because he gives a reason in this poem why. And you've seen pictures of how much he loves us, and stretches out his arms. And I like hugs.
<v Martha>我遇到了这位朋友，她寄给我这个关于上帝为什么创造拥抱的书签。我不记得具体内容，但我让女儿在电脑上把它放大并放在卷轴上。我把它缝在运动衫上。我告诉人们我没发明拥抱，是上帝发明的，因为他在诗里给出了理由。你们看过上帝有多爱我们、伸出双臂的图片。而我喜欢拥抱。

00:33:30.750 --> 00:33:44.000
<v Liz Weil>Martha tells me she's a recovering alcoholic. She tells me that one of her children, her daughter, died in 1981. She works evenings at Kmart, because she can't stand to spend her nights alone.
<v Liz Weil>玛莎告诉我她是康复中的酒鬼。她告诉我，她的一个孩子，她女儿，在1981年去世了。她在Kmart上夜班，因为她无法忍受独自度过夜晚。

00:33:44.000 --> 00:34:04.530
<v Martha>It seems like I've had so many bad things happen in my life that now something good is happening, meeting these people. My friend in Florida thinks I ought to go to AA and tell my story, Because she said, Martha, you've been through wars.
<v Martha>似乎我生活中发生过太多糟糕的事，现在终于有些好事发生了，遇到了这些人。我在佛罗里达的朋友觉得我应该去参加AA并讲述我的故事，因为她说，玛莎，你经历过战争。

00:34:04.530 --> 00:34:29.900
<v Liz Weil>Martha goes on to tell me that before SIA events, she gets so excited that she worries that she'll have a heart attack or a stroke. People spend a lot of time getting ready for Saturday night's banquet. Women wear dresses with slits and sequins. Men wear jackets, bolo ties. This is the big event, the final showdown. The evening starts with a prayer.
<v Liz Weil>玛莎接着告诉我，在SIA活动之前，她太兴奋了，以至于她担心自己会心脏病发作或中风。人们花费大量时间准备周六晚上的宴会。女士们穿着有开叉和亮片的裙子。男士们穿着夹克、波洛领带。这是重头戏，最后的摊牌。晚上以祈祷开始。

00:34:29.900 --> 00:35:05.460
<v Sia Speaker 2>We take pause before you God, acknowledging we can rest quietly in your arms, with confidence and assurance of your love, grace, and mercy, as we walk through this season called singledness, as we ask, hope, and believe all things in the name of Jesus Christ, Amen. OK, flag bearers, here we go.
<v Sia Speaker 2>我们在上帝面前停顿，承认我们可以安静地在你的怀里休息，对你的爱、恩典和怜悯充满信心和保证，当我们走过这个被称为单身的季节时，正如我们以耶稣基督的名祈祷、希望和相信一切一样，阿门。好了，旗手们，我们走吧。

00:35:05.460 --> 00:35:57.670
<v Liz Weil>What's going on here is really rather incredible. There are 50 men and women lined up in long aisles by twos. Each person is bearing a flimsy, nylon handkerchief-sized flag on a long, thin pole. It would be campy if people's eyes weren't so reverent, so serious. They march towards the front of the room. After dinner, a man gives a very boring talk on Russian agriculture. Plaques are awarded to old board members. Election results are announced for the new. At the end of the banquet, Eldon, the outgoing president, gets up to make a speech. He's good-looking, jovial, in his 40s. He beats the podium nervously with his hand. A smile creeps across his face.
<v Liz Weil>这里发生的事真是太不可思议了。有50名男士和女士在长长的过道里排成两行。每个人都拿着一面廉价的、尼龙手帕大小的旗子，杆子又长又细。如果不是人们的眼神那么虔诚、那么严肃，这会很俗气。他们向房间前方前进。晚饭后，一名男子对俄罗斯农业发表了一场非常无聊的演讲。奖章颁发给了旧董事会成员。宣布了新任人员的选举结果。宴会结束时，即将卸任的主席埃尔登站起来发表讲话。他长相英俊，快活，40多岁。他紧张地用手敲打着讲台。一丝微笑浮现在他的脸上。

00:35:57.670 --> 00:36:53.230
<v Eldon>So I guess I got one more thing I got to say, or I'm going to be a dead duck before this night is over. A year ago, I could not find hired help to go on harvest. I was ticked off, I don't mind telling you. My income was just plummeting like a rock. Well, I got to go to Indiana. We got a board meeting. And I'm not harvesting, so I don't have an excuse. I met somebody there that, she probably won't like this, but at the time, didn't really help my mood that much. Fortunately, she didn't give up, and she stayed in contact with me. So you people, you got to remember, I've been in this thing a long time. And I guess I wouldn't say that I had given up, but I really wasn't searching that hard. And I found somebody, and it's going to result in a wedding dance April the 5, and anybody--
<v Eldon>所以我猜我还有一件事要说，否则今晚结束前我就得死掉了。一年前，我找不到帮手去收割。我气坏了，不瞒你们说。我的收入像石头一样直线下滑。好吧，我去印第安纳州参加董事会会议。我没在收割，所以我没借口。我在那儿遇到了一个人，她可能不喜欢这样，但当时，并没有真正改善我的心情。幸运的是，她没有放弃，她和我保持联系。所以各位，你们得记住，我参加这活动很久了。我猜我不能说我已经放弃了，但我确实没那么努力地在寻找。但我找到了某人，这将在4月5日举行婚礼舞会，任何人——

00:36:53.230 --> 00:36:59.690
<v Eldon>[APPLAUSE]
<v Eldon>[掌声]

00:36:59.690 --> 00:37:20.960
<v Liz Weil>Everybody stands up and claps and hollers for what seems like a good 10 minutes. People are so exquisitely happy, wistful, jealous, and proud. The scene feels oddly like the end of a Miss America Pageant. Women cry and clasp hands over their mouths. When the ovation fades, they gather around the bride-to-be.
<v Liz Weil>每个人都站起来拍手、欢呼，持续了大概足足10分钟。人们是那么精致地快乐、渴望、嫉妒和自豪。这一幕感觉莫名其妙地像“美国小姐”选美大赛的结局。女人们哭泣着，双手合十放在嘴边。当欢呼声减弱时，她们围拢在新娘周围。

00:37:20.960 --> 00:37:24.120
<v Sia Woman 6>[UNINTELLIGIBLE] I'm so proud. I'm so glad.
<v Sia Woman 6>[难以辨认] 我太骄傲了。我太高兴了。

00:37:24.120 --> 00:37:25.890
<v Eldon's Fiancee>So am I. I'm so happy.
<v Eldon's Fiancee>我也是。我太幸福了。

00:37:25.890 --> 00:37:27.380
<v Sia Woman 6>I bet you are.
<v Sia Woman 6>我敢说你是。

00:37:27.380 --> 00:37:29.050
<v Sia Woman 7>I'll bet you are. Whoo hoo.
<v Sia Woman 7>我敢说你是。呜呼。

00:37:29.050 --> 00:37:35.320
<v Liz Weil>In the crowd, I see Martha looking overwhelmed. She embraces Eldon's fiancee.
<v Liz Weil>在人群中，我看到玛莎显得不知所措。她拥抱了埃尔登的未婚妻。

00:37:35.320 --> 00:37:41.160
<v Martha>Oh, quite a shock. Oh, but you're so lucky.
